---
// The body of /inference/: the sky behind its hero, what the engine is, the numbers, the five figures,
// the token loop, the models and formats, and a quickstart that ends in the inference docs.
import BenchChart from "./BenchChart.astro"
import BenchSummary from "./BenchSummary.astro"
import ModelsFormats from "./ModelsFormats.astro"
import Recording from "./Recording.astro"
import BandwidthBound from "../figures/bandwidth-bound/BandwidthBound.astro"
import QdotMapping from "../figures/qdot-mapping/QdotMapping.astro"
import ExpertCache from "../figures/expert-cache/ExpertCache.astro"
import ElasticCache from "../figures/elastic-cache/ElasticCache.astro"
import HadamardRotation from "../figures/hadamard-rotation/HadamardRotation.astro"
import TokenLoop from "../figures/token-loop/TokenLoop.astro"
import InferenceHero from "./hero/InferenceHero.astro"
import { REPO_URL } from "../../lib/bench/load"

const SERVE_COMMAND = "phobos-cli --gguf minicpm5-1b-Q8_0.gguf --listen 127.0.0.1:8080"
const BUILD_COMMAND = "cargo build --release --features cuda -p phobos-cli"
const PROMPT_COMMAND = 'phobos-cli --gguf minicpm5-1b-Q8_0.gguf "Why is the sky dark at night?"'
---

<InferenceHero />
<div class="inference">
  <section class="inf-section" aria-labelledby="engine">
    <h2 id="engine">What it is</h2>
    <div class="inf-split">
      <ul class="inf-points">
        <li>
          <h3>GGUF models on Phobos kernels</h3>
          <p>
            <code>phobos-cli</code> loads a GGUF file, picks the forward pass from the architecture it names, and runs
            every layer on kernels written in Phobos and compiled for the card. Decode fuses a chain of layer stages
            into one persistent kernel.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>An OpenAI-compatible server</h3>
          <p>
            <code>--listen</code> serves <code>/v1/chat/completions</code>, <code>/v1/completions</code> and
            <code>/v1/models</code>, renders conversations and tools with the model's own chat template, streams chat
            over server-sent events, and puts a dashboard on the terminal.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Made for little VRAM</h3>
          <p>
            Written and measured on an 8 GB card, on which two 27B models at under two bits a weight fit with room for
            their context.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Experts streamed from host memory</h3>
          <p>
            A mixture-of-experts model that does not fit keeps a cache of experts on the card and computes the misses
            on the CPU, with no manual split between the two. The cache gives memory back when another program fills the
            card, and takes it again once it is free.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <Recording name="phobos-cli" label="phobos-cli starting with --listen and drawing its dashboard" widthPx={960} heightPx={540}>
        <code>phobos-cli --listen</code>: the splash, then the dashboard with the model, the memory on the card, the
        network of layers and the log.
      </Recording>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="inf-section" aria-labelledby="numbers">
    <h2 id="numbers">Decode and prefill against llama.cpp</h2>
    <BenchSummary />
    <BenchChart />
    <p class="inf-more">
      How these runs were measured, the 35B on a replayed coding-agent session, and what happens when another program
      takes part of the card: <a href="/docs/reference/benchmarks/">Benchmarks</a>. To measure your own card:{" "}
      <a href="/docs/inference/benchmarking/">Benchmarking</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="inf-section" aria-labelledby="why">
    <h2 id="why">Five ideas behind the numbers</h2>

    <div class="inf-figure">
      <div class="inf-figure-text">
        <h3>Decode waits on memory</h3>
        <p>
          A decode step reads every weight once and does one multiply-add with it. That is about two operations per
          byte, and the card needs roughly ten times that before its math units, not its memory bus, set the pace. A
          prompt reuses each weight for every token in it, and the bottleneck flips. Decode kernels are written to
          keep the bus busy, not the math units.
        </p>
      </div>
      <BandwidthBound />
    </div>

    <div class="inf-figure">
      <div class="inf-figure-text">
        <h3>One warp per output</h3>
        <p>
          Every output of a decode matrix-vector product is one row of weights dotted with the activation. Giving each
          thread its own row scatters a warp's reads over 32 rows. The lanes matvecs give a whole warp one row instead:
          its 32 lanes read one contiguous run, and <code>sum&lt;over ctx.lane&gt;</code> folds their partial sums
          in five shuffle stages.
        </p>
      </div>
      <QdotMapping />
    </div>

    <div class="inf-figure">
      <div class="inf-figure-text">
        <h3>A model that does not fit</h3>
        <p>
          Qwen3.6-35B-A3B routes every token to 8 of 256 experts per layer, and its experts alone do not fit on the
          card. Phobos keeps the experts in host memory and a cache of them on the card, least recently
          used out first. On a decode step the CPU computes the misses straight from host memory while the card runs
          the hits; a prompt copies its misses over PCIe.
        </p>
      </div>
      <ExpertCache />
    </div>

    <div class="inf-figure">
      <div class="inf-figure-text">
        <h3>Giving the memory back</h3>
        <p>
          The cache is sized from the card's free memory, and Phobos reads the whole card's free memory again between
          passes. When another program fills the card, the cache shrinks instead of letting the driver page buffers to
          host memory, and more of the misses go to the CPU threads. When the memory is free again, the cache grows
          back. llama.cpp fixes its split at load time, so the same squeeze pages its buffers and every token reads them
          back over PCIe.
        </p>
      </div>
      <ElasticCache id="fig-inference-elastic-cache" />
    </div>

    <div class="inf-figure">
      <div class="inf-figure-text">
        <h3>Rotate before rounding</h3>
        <p>
          Ternary weights share one scale per block, so one outlier can ruin it: the best scale fits the outlier and
          the small weights round to zero. A Hadamard rotation spreads the outlier over the block before rounding. The
          activations get the same rotation at run time, so the product does not change, and one rotation serves every
          projection that reads the same input.
        </p>
      </div>
      <HadamardRotation />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="inf-section" aria-labelledby="loop">
    <h2 id="loop">One token at a time</h2>
    <div class="inf-split is-figure">
      <p>
        Decoding is a loop: the tokenizer turns the prompt into tokens, the layers turn the last token into logits,
        the sampler picks the next token, and that token streams out and goes back in. Every pass reads all the
        weights the token needs from the card's memory, which is why the ideas above matter.
      </p>
      <TokenLoop id="fig-inference-token-loop" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="inf-section" aria-labelledby="models">
    <h2 id="models">Models and formats</h2>
    <p>
      The architectures are <code>llama</code>, <code>qwen35</code>, <code>qwen35moe</code> and <code>glm4</code>.
      These files have been checked against llama.cpp, token for token where greedy decoding is stable. Every format
      below decodes on the card; see <a href="/docs/inference/models-and-formats/">Models and formats</a> for what each
      architecture covers.
    </p>
    <ModelsFormats />
  </section>

  <section class="inf-section" aria-labelledby="quickstart">
    <h2 id="quickstart">Quickstart</h2>
    <ol class="inf-steps">
      <li>
        <h3>Get the binary</h3>
        <p>
          Download a <a href={`${REPO_URL}/releases/latest`}>release</a> and keep <code>kernel-cache/</code> beside the
          binaries, or build from a checkout:
        </p>
        <pre><code>{BUILD_COMMAND}</code></pre>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Ask a question</h3>
        <p>
          Download <a href="https://huggingface.co/Abiray/MiniCPM5-1B-GGUF">MiniCPM5-1B-Q8_0</a> and pass a prompt.
          Without one, <code>phobos-cli</code> starts a REPL.
        </p>
        <pre><code>{PROMPT_COMMAND}</code></pre>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Serve it</h3>
        <p>Point any OpenAI client at <code>http://127.0.0.1:8080/v1</code>.</p>
        <pre><code>{SERVE_COMMAND}</code></pre>
      </li>
    </ol>
    <ul class="inf-links">
      <li><a href="/docs/inference/">Quickstart</a></li>
      <li><a href="/docs/inference/models-and-formats/">Models and formats</a></li>
      <li><a href="/docs/inference/serving/">Serving</a></li>
      <li><a href="/docs/inference/sampling/">Sampling</a></li>
      <li><a href="/docs/inference/benchmarking/">Benchmarking</a></li>
      <li><a href="/docs/inference/kernel-cache/">Kernel cache</a></li>
      <li><a href="/docs/inference/troubleshooting/">Troubleshooting</a></li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</div>

<style>
  .inference {
    display: grid;
    gap: clamp(3.5rem, 8vw, 6rem);
    margin-top: 1rem;
  }

  .inf-section > :global(* + *) {
    margin-top: 1rem;
  }

  .inf-section h2 {
    font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 3vw, 2rem);
  }

  .inf-section h3 {
    font-size: 1.0625rem;
  }

  .inf-split {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 5fr) minmax(0, 7fr);
    gap: 2rem 3rem;
    align-items: start;
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }

  .inf-points {
    display: grid;
    gap: 1.25rem;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .inf-points li {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .inf-points h3 {
    margin: 0 0 0.375rem;
  }

  .inf-points p {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--dust);
  }

  .inf-more {
    color: var(--dust);
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
  }

  .inf-figure {
    margin-top: 3rem;
  }

  .inf-figure-text {
    max-width: 46rem;
  }

  .inf-figure-text p {
    margin-top: 0.5rem;
  }

  .inf-figure :global(.chart) {
    margin-top: 1.25rem;
  }

  .inf-split.is-figure {
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 4fr) minmax(0, 8fr);
  }

  .inf-split.is-figure :global(.chart) {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .inf-steps {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 1.5rem 2rem;
    margin: 1.5rem 0 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .inf-steps li {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    min-width: 0;
    margin: 0;
    padding-top: 1rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--sky-line);
  }

  .inf-steps h3,
  .inf-steps p {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .inf-steps pre {
    margin: auto 0 0;
    padding: 0.75rem;
    overflow-x: auto;
    border: 1px solid var(--sky-line);
    background: var(--sky-1);
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    white-space: pre-wrap;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  .inf-links {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem 1.5rem;
    margin: 1.5rem 0 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .inf-links li {
    margin: 0;
  }

  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .inf-split,
    .inf-split.is-figure,
    .inf-steps {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    }
  }
</style>
